# Minimal CRUD API

## Quick Start

1. Build and run:

   dotnet run

2. Open Swagger UI (development environment):

   https://localhost:{port}/swagger

3. API base path:

   `https://localhost:{port}/api/entity1`

## Endpoints

- GET /api/entity1
  - Returns list of Entity1 objects
  - Example:
    curl -sS https://localhost:{port}/api/entity1

- GET /api/entity1/{id}
  - Returns single Entity1 or 404
  - Example:
    curl -sS https://localhost:{port}/api/entity1/1

- POST /api/entity1
  - Create new Entity1. Body JSON example:
    {
      "name": "Example"
    }
  - Example:
    curl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"name":"Example"}' https://localhost:{port}/api/entity1

- PUT /api/entity1/{id}
  - Update existing Entity1. Body JSON same as POST.
  - Example:
    curl -X PUT -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"name":"Updated"}' https://localhost:{port}/api/entity1/1

- DELETE /api/entity1/{id}
  - Deletes entity
  - Example:
    curl -X DELETE https://localhost:{port}/api/entity1/1

## Important Notes

- Persistence: This template uses the EF Core In-Memory provider by default. Data does NOT persist across process restarts. This is intended for development and demos only.

- Upgrade to a persistent provider: To make data persistent, replace the In-Memory registration in `Program.cs` with a relational provider (for example SQL Server or PostgreSQL), add the corresponding EF Core provider package(s), and enable migrations.

- Validation: Basic validation attributes are applied to the `Entity1` model. The `[ApiController]` attribute provides automatic model validation and will return 400 Bad Request when input is invalid.

- Swagger/OpenAPI is enabled for easy exploration. Include example requests in your client documentation or Postman collection.

